        <h2>Foundation Cracks and Gaps Along the Sill Plate</h2>

        <p>
            Concrete and masonry shift slowly as Toronto soil freezes and thaws
            each year. That movement can open thin cracks where the concrete
            foundation meets the wooden frame. The sill plate is the wood board
            that rests on top of the foundation. Any gap under or beside it can
            lead mice into basement ceiling voids. Crumbling mortar joints near
            the ground give mice another easy way into wall cavities. Check the
            outside edge first, because gaps here often hide behind soil and plants.
        </p>


        <h2>Gaps Around Plumbing, Gas, Electrical and AC Lines</h2>

        <p>
            Every pipe or cable that crosses an outside wall needs a hole cut for
            it. Installers often leave a ring shaped gap between the pipe and the
            wall. Air conditioner line sets and gas meters often hide these gaps
            behind equipment. Inside, the same gaps open into wall cavities behind
            kitchen and bathroom plumbing. Never open gas, electrical or mechanical
            equipment, and call a licensed tradesperson for repairs.
        </p>


        <h2>Garage Door Bottom Seals and Exterior Door Thresholds</h2>

        <p>
            A closed garage door still leaves a thin gap along the bottom edge.
            Rubber seals harden in cold weather, then crack and shrink after several
            winters. Uneven concrete creates larger gaps, especially at the two
            bottom corners of the door. Stand inside the garage on a bright day and
            look for any daylight. Mice that enter here can reach the house through
            the door to the garage. Exterior door thresholds fail the same way when
            sweeps wear thin or frames shift.
        </p>


        <h2>Brick Weep Holes, Siding Gaps and Wall Cavities</h2>

        <p>
            Brick veneer walls have a row of small slots near the bottom, called
            weep holes. These slots let trapped water drain out of the gap behind
            the brick. Mice can enter that gap through them, but it does not always
            reach rooms. Never fill weep holes with mortar or caulk, because trapped
            water damages the wall. Use stainless steel weep hole guards that let
            water and air pass through. Siding gaps and brick joints are not drainage
            paths, so you can close them.
        </p>


        <h2>Dryer Vents, Exhaust Openings and Basement Windows</h2>

        <p>
            Dryer and bath fan covers often crack, bend or lose their small flaps.
            A damaged cover gives mice a short, warm tunnel into ducts and nearby
            voids. Basement window frames also shrink, crack and pull away from
            the foundation over time. Window wells collect leaves, which give mice
            cover while they test the frame. Do not cover a dryer vent with fine
            mesh, because lint clogs it quickly. A blocked vent raises fire risk,
            so choose a guard the dryer maker approves.
        </p>


        <h2>Roof Soffits, Fascia and Attic Vent Openings</h2>

        <p>
            Mice climb well, and rough brick, downspouts and cables all work as
            ladders. They can reach soffits, fascia boards, roof returns and attic
            vents from the wall. Tree branches near the roof also give them a quick
            bridge to the house. So an inspection that stops at eye level will miss
            these high gaps. Use binoculars from the ground, and never climb a roof
            or ladder alone. Leave rooftop checks to professionals who carry proper
            fall protection and safety gear.
        </p>


