        <h2>Why Do Mice Enter Toronto Homes More Often During Fall and Winter?</h2>


        <h3>How Falling Temperatures Change Mouse Shelter Seeking Behaviour</h3>

        <p>
            Mice feel the cold more than larger animals, because their small bodies
            lose heat fast. By October, Toronto nights turn cold, and wind and rain
            make open ground harsh. Seeds and insects also become scarcer outdoors,
            so mice search for steadier food nearby. Snow cover hides remaining food
            and removes the shelter that tall grass once gave. A warm house offers
            heat, dry nesting spots and less wind in one place.
        </p>


        <h3>Why Attached Garages, Basements and Wall Voids Provide Winter Shelter</h3>

        <p>
            Garages, basements and wall voids share three traits that mice value
            highly in winter. They stay quiet, they hold heat, and they contain
            soft materials for nests. Stored cardboard, old fabric and paper boxes
            quickly turn into ready made bedding for mice. Insulated walls keep
            nests warm, while people stay busy in other parts of the house. Pet
            food or birdseed stored in a garage adds a reliable meal near that shelter.
        </p>


        <h3>Why Mice Can Remain Active Inside Throughout Winter</h3>

        <p>
            House mice do not hibernate, so they stay busy inside your home all
            winter long. A female mouse can become pregnant again very soon after
            giving birth indoors. Pregnancy lasts about three weeks, and young mice
            mature within a couple of months. So a few mice in November can become
            a much larger crowd by spring. Watch for fresh droppings, night noises
            and chewed food packages during the coldest months.
        </p>


        <h2>Can Mice Enter a Toronto Home in Summer Too?</h2>

        <p>
            Yes, mice can enter a Toronto home in spring, summer or any other
            season. Construction, landscaping and garden changes can push mice
            toward buildings in warm months. Two different problems exist here,
            and homeowners often confuse them with each other. One is seasonal
            movement, where outdoor mice move into the building as weather cools.
            The other is an indoor population that settled earlier and keeps
            breeding all year. Sealing helps the first problem, but the second
            needs trapping and monitoring too.
        </p>


        <h2>How Can You Tell Where Mice Are Actually Getting In?</h2>


        <div class="info-card">

            <h3>Fresh Droppings, Gnaw Marks and Grease Marks Near Openings</h3>

            <p>
                Fresh droppings look dark, soft and shiny, while old ones look grey
                and dry. Mouse droppings are small and rice shaped, usually near
                three to six millimetres long. Gnaw marks show fresh pale wood, and
                grease marks show dark smudges along edges. Grease marks come from
                oils on their fur as they rub past surfaces. One sign alone does not
                confirm the entry point, because mice travel far inside walls. Look
                for several signs together near the same gap before you trust your
                guess.
            </p>

        </div>


        <div class="info-card">

            <h3>Mouse Tracks and Repeated Movement Along Walls</h3>

            <p>
                Mice follow walls and repeat the same paths, which leaves faint
                trails over time. A thin layer of flour or baby powder can reveal
                footprints and tail drags. Put it in a shallow tray or strip along
                a wall, away from food. Check the powder each morning with a
                flashlight held low to the floor. Do not blow on the dust or sweep
                it dry, because dust can carry germs. Photograph the marks, then
                wipe the area with a damp towel while wearing gloves.
            </p>

        </div>


        <div class="info-card">

            <h3>Scratching in Walls vs Mice Entering Through the Roof</h3>

            <p>
                Sound tells you where mice travel, but not always where they came
                in. Light scratching in walls at night often points to mice moving
                between floors. Sounds above the ceiling may come from the attic,
                yet the opening could be low. Mice can enter at the foundation and
                climb inside the wall to the attic. Heavy thumping, daytime noise
                or loud chewing suggests squirrels, raccoons or rats instead.
                Wildlife needs different handling, so ask a qualified professional
                to identify the animal.
            </p>

        </div>


        <h2>How to Inspect the Exterior of a Toronto Home Step by Step</h2>

        <ol class="inspection-list">

            <li>
                Walk the outside of your home in daylight with a flashlight and
                gloves. Stay on the ground and keep away from gas meters, wiring
                and mechanical equipment.
            </li>

            <li>
                Inspect the foundation perimeter for cracks, soil gaps and damaged
                mortar near the ground.
            </li>

            <li>
                Check utility penetrations and the attached garage, including side
                seals and bottom corners.
            </li>

            <li>
                Examine doors, windows and vents for worn sweeps, torn screens and
                loose covers.
            </li>

            <li>
                Inspect brick, siding and visible roof junctions from the ground
                using binoculars.
            </li>

            <li>
                Photograph suspicious gaps and any droppings, rub marks or gnawed
                material you find.
            </li>

            <li>
                Check the matching interior area, such as the basement wall or
                under the sink.
            </li>

            <li>
                Arrange further inspection for inaccessible or uncertain locations,
                rather than guessing.
            </li>

            <li>
                Label each gap <strong>confirmed</strong> if fresh signs exist
                nearby, and <strong>potential</strong> if they do not.
            </li>

        </ol>


        <h2>Which Toronto Home Types Are Most Vulnerable to Mouse Entry?</h2>

        <p>
            Toronto has many building styles, and each style has its own weak spots.
            This section describes common building features, and it makes no claims
            about any neighbourhood.
        </p>


        <h3>Older Brick and Semi Detached Toronto Houses</h3>

        <p>
            Older brick houses often have worn mortar joints, settled foundations
            and decades of repairs. Each renovation can add new pipes, wires or
            ducts through the original walls. Those added holes are not always
            sealed as tightly as they should be. Semi detached homes share a party
            wall, so cavities may connect between neighbours. Neighbours should
            talk early, because one sealed home can push mice toward the other.
        </p>


        <h3>Newer Townhouses and Attached Garages</h3>

        <p>
            Newer townhouses have tighter walls, but they still have many
            construction joints and seams. Builders run pipes, wires and ducts
            through shared floors, walls and service chases. Gaps around these
            runs can sometimes link one unit to the next unit. Attached garages
            add door seals, expansion joints and side doors that wear quickly.
            Start with the garage, because it connects the outdoors to the living
            space.
        </p>


        <h3>Basement Apartments and Multi Unit Rental Properties</h3>

        <p>
            Basement apartments sit at the lowest level, where mice often find
            gaps first. Pipes, drains and laundry lines cross walls and ceilings
            shared with other areas. In multi unit buildings, shared garbage rooms
            and service shafts can feed several homes. Ontario's Residential
            Tenancies Act requires landlords to keep rental units in good repair.
            Tenants should report mouse problems in writing and keep dated copies
            of every request. Coordinated inspections work best, because treating
            one unit alone often leaves mice in neighbouring spaces.
        </p>


