        <h2>How to Seal Mouse Entry Points Without Creating Other Building Problems</h2>


        <h3>Which Materials Work for Mouse Exclusion?</h3>

        <p>
            Good exclusion materials are hard, durable and tough for small teeth
            to chew. Stainless steel mesh and galvanized hardware cloth with
            quarter inch openings suit larger gaps. Sheet metal flashing covers
            damaged corners, and cement mortar repairs cracks in masonry and
            concrete. Copper mesh packed into small holes resists rust better
            than plain steel wool.
        </p>

        <p>
            Use exterior grade sealant only to lock metal in place, not as the
            barrier. Expanding foam alone fails, because mice chew soft foam and
            can tunnel through it. Pest management guidance from University of
            California IPM also warns against relying on soft fillers. Rodent
            resistant door sweeps with metal or firm brush edges seal gaps under
            doors.
        </p>


        <div class="warning-box">

            <strong>Important:</strong>

            <p>
                Some openings serve an important ventilation or drainage function.
                Do not block functional vents, exhaust outlets or brick weep holes.
                Use appropriate guards and follow manufacturer and local requirements.
            </p>

        </div>


        <h3>Which Openings Should Never Be Completely Blocked?</h3>

        <p>
            Some openings protect your home, so mouse guards must never stop their
            job. Never fully block brick weep holes, dryer vents, bath fan outlets
            or furnace exhausts. High efficiency furnaces and water heaters often
            use plastic pipes for intake and exhaust. Blocking those pipes can cause
            carbon monoxide to build up inside your home. In Ontario, ask a licensed
            gas technician about any guard near fuel burning appliances. Always
            follow the appliance maker's instructions and local building rules for
            every vent guard.
        </p>


        <h3>Should You Seal Entry Points While Mice Are Still Inside?</h3>

        <p>
            Sealing too early can trap live mice inside walls, floors or attic
            spaces. Trapped mice may die in places you cannot reach, which causes
            strong odours. First, confirm whether mice live inside by checking
            fresh droppings and night activity. If activity is present, start
            trapping before you close the main entry points. Some professionals
            seal in stages, keeping one monitored route open until trapping ends.
            After traps stop catching mice, seal the last route and keep monitoring
            for weeks.
        </p>


        <h3>Why Mice Return After Poorly Sealed Repairs</h3>

        <p>
            Mice return when a repair leaves even one small gap open somewhere
            nearby. They also chew weak materials, loosen sealant and widen cracks
            over several weeks. Freeze and thaw cycles can pull caulk away from
            brick, wood and concrete. Mice that stayed inside the walls can also
            find a new way out and in. Food left in garages and basements keeps
            them close and interested in your property. Inspect every repair again
            after two to four weeks to catch these failures.
        </p>


