        <h2>What Should You Do If Mice Have Already Entered Your House?</h2>


        <h3>Confirm Whether the Infestation Is Active</h3>

        <p>
            Do not assume mice are active just because you found a few old
            droppings. Clean the area safely, then watch for new droppings over
            three to five nights. Place monitoring stations or snap traps in safe
            spots along walls, away from children. Repeat sightings, fresh gnawing
            and caught mice all confirm that the problem is active. Write down
            dates and locations, because this record helps any professional who
            visits later.
        </p>


        <h3>Reduce Accessible Food, Water and Nesting Materials</h3>

        <p>
            Mice need very little food, so small leftovers can keep them alive for
            weeks. Store pantry items, pet food and birdseed in glass, metal or
            thick plastic bins. Use garbage bins with tight lids, and take kitchen
            waste out each night. Fix leaking taps and wipe wet sink areas, because
            mice also need regular water. Replace cardboard storage boxes with
            plastic bins, and clear clutter from basements and garages. Move
            woodpiles and dense plants a short distance away from the foundation
            when possible.
        </p>


        <h3>Coordinate Mouse Control With Exterior Exclusion</h3>

        <p>
            Good control uses three steps together: reduce mice, close entry points
            and monitor results. Snap traps and enclosed trap stations catch mice
            when placed along their travel routes. Check traps every day, and wear
            gloves whenever you handle them or caught mice. In Canada, Health
            Canada's PMRA registers rodenticides, and each label sets strict
            directions for use. Tamper resistant bait stations help protect
            children and pets, so professionals should place them. No plan removes
            every mouse overnight, so expect several weeks of follow up checks.
        </p>


        <h3>Handle Mouse Droppings and Contaminated Materials Safely</h3>

        <p>
            Mouse droppings can carry germs, so careful cleanup matters far more
            than speed. Health Canada guidance links rodent droppings to illness
            risk when dust gets into the air. Never dry sweep or vacuum droppings,
            because both actions lift dust into the air. Air out the room for 30
            minutes, then wear rubber gloves and a fitted mask. Spray droppings
            with disinfectant until wet, wait five minutes, then wipe with paper
            towels. Seal waste in a bag, wash your hands well, and discard
            contaminated food.
        </p>


