        <h3>When Can a Homeowner Handle Mouse Proofing?</h3>

        <p>
            A homeowner can handle low risk jobs that need no ladder or special
            tools. Replacing a worn door sweep or garage bottom seal is a good
            example. Small, visible cracks in masonry away from utilities also
            suit careful DIY repair. Storing food properly and clearing clutter
            are easy tasks that help every household. Call a professional if you
            find several gaps or fresh droppings.
        </p>


        <h3>When Should You Hire a Mouse Control Professional?</h3>

        <p>
            Hire a professional when signs keep returning after you have tried
            basic steps. Persistent droppings, constant noises and damaged attic
            insulation point to a bigger problem. Multiple entry points, roofline
            gaps and openings near utilities also need trained eyes. Shared walls
            and multi unit buildings require coordination that one homeowner
            cannot easily manage. A trained technician can also judge whether
            sealing should wait until trapping ends.
        </p>


        <h3>What Should a Toronto Mouse Inspection Include?</h3>

        <p>
            A strong inspection starts outside and covers the foundation, garage,
            utilities and visible roofline. Inside, the inspector should check
            basements, attics, kitchens and areas with recent activity. Good
            reports include photos of each entry point and a clear map of mouse
            activity. You should also receive control recommendations, an
            exclusion scope and a written quote. Ask about follow up visits,
            monitoring plans and any limits on the service. Not every company
            offers every service, so confirm each detail before you book.
        </p>


        <h2>How to Prevent Mice From Entering Again Next Fall</h2>


        <h3>Late Summer and Early Fall Exterior Inspection</h3>

        <p>
            Inspect your home in August or September, well before Toronto nights
            turn cold. This timing gives you weeks to repair gaps without rushing
            in bad weather. Test garage seals, check vent covers and look at mortar
            and caulk condition. Trim branches that touch the roof, and clear tall
            plants from the foundation edge. Book any roof or high repairs early,
            because contractors often get busy in autumn.
        </p>


        <h3>Garage, Basement and Food Storage Maintenance</h3>

        <p>
            Garages and basements need a short checking routine every month during
            cold weather. Keep pet food and birdseed in sealed metal bins, stored
            well off the floor. Shelves raise stored items and let you see droppings
            along the wall edges easily. Swap cardboard boxes for plastic bins, and
            keep firewood outside, away from walls. Repair leaks quickly, and check
            door thresholds whenever you notice daylight at the edges.
        </p>


        <h3>Winter Monitoring After Exclusion</h3>

        <p>
            A quiet home does not always prove that the mice are truly gone. Mice
            are mostly active at night, so you may never see one directly. Check
            traps, monitoring stations and nontoxic tracking powder once a week
            through the winter. Record the date, the location and what you found
            during each check. Look closely at sealed areas for fresh gnawing, new
            droppings or loose repair materials.
        </p>


        <h3>When to Reinspect Repairs</h3>

        <p>
            Recheck repairs after any event that can move or damage your building
            envelope. Renovations, new utility work, siding repairs and vent
            replacements can create fresh gaps. Severe storms and freeze and thaw
            cycles can also crack mortar and loosen seals. Inspect again whenever
            you notice new droppings, new gnawing or unusual night noises. A yearly
            professional review adds a second opinion on repairs you cannot reach.
        </p>


